  <h2>Why Traditional Buyers Run Into a Wall Here</h2>
  <p>A buyer using conventional financing, FHA, or VA can't close on a home with unresolved liens. Their lender requires clean title — that's a hard underwriting requirement, not a preference. Even a buyer who loves your house and has a pre-approval letter loses financing the moment the title search surfaces a lien.</p>
  <p>That leaves you with two realistic options: find a cash buyer who doesn't need lender approval, or pay off the lien yourself before listing. Most people in this situation can't do the second one. If you had the cash to settle the lien outright, it probably wouldn't have reached this point.</p>
  <p>I don't use a bank. I don't have an underwriter reviewing your title. I can make you an offer without any of that machinery in the way.</p>

  <h2>Property Tax vs. IRS vs. Judgment Liens — What's the Difference?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sedgwick County property tax liens</strong> — Most common in Wichita. Miss a year of county property taxes and the Sedgwick County Treasurer records a lien. After three years of continuous delinquency, they can pursue foreclosure through Sedgwick County District Court.</li>
    <li><strong>Federal IRS tax liens</strong> — Recorded with the Sedgwick County Register of Deeds on North Main Street. Must be discharged, paid, or subordinated before closing. Coordination takes more time but isn't a dealbreaker.</li>
    <li><strong>Kansas state tax liens</strong> — The Kansas Department of Revenue records these and they surface on title searches. Same closing process as IRS liens.</li>
    <li><strong>Judgment liens</strong> — Sometimes a surprise. A court judgment against you personally can attach to property you own in Sedgwick County. Workable, just requires payoff coordination at closing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How I Actually Buy Houses with Liens in Wichita</h2>
  <p>We talk first. You tell me what you know about the lien — the type, roughly how much is owed, how long it's been there. If you don't know the exact figures, that's fine. The title company will surface everything recorded at the Sedgwick County Register of Deeds during the transaction.</p>
  <p>I look at the property and make you a cash offer. The offer accounts for the home's condition, the Wichita market, and the outstanding obligations. I'm not going to offer you full market value — I'm a cash buyer, not a retail buyer — but I'll show you the math and explain how I got there.</p>
  <p>If you accept, we open escrow with a title company. The title company contacts each lienholder directly: the Sedgwick County Treasurer's office for property tax delinquencies, the IRS or KDOR for federal or state liens, whatever else has been recorded. They get official payoff figures, wire those amounts at closing, collect lien releases, and record the releases with the county. By the time the deed transfers to me, the liens are gone from the title.</p>
  <p>You get what's left of the proceeds. Then it's done.</p>

